+# Dual-layout metric peepholes: parallel evaluation
+
+Base: `91bbcda81fbcb9750298f9869fb5b990d9d819f5` (2026-10-21).
+
+## Problem
+
+At `-O1`, static-chain dual-layout exploration emits the chain and original
+orders and runs the full x86 text peephole on each before choosing the smaller
+result. The candidates are independent, but they were evaluated serially. This
+is part of MS-20's measured compile-time hotspot; changing to a raw instruction
+count is not valid because `zstd_count` reverses its preferred order only after
+the peephole.
+
+## Change and boundaries
+
+`ArchCodegen::peephole_pair_for_metric` preserves a serial default. x86-64
+overrides it with a helper that evaluates one candidate on a scoped worker and
+the other on the caller. It falls back to serial evaluation below 8 KiB of
+combined input or when the process has only one available hardware thread.
+There is never more than one worker, and candidate order and panic propagation
+are explicit. Whole-file peephole ordering, candidate scoring, generated
+assembly, and all non-x86 backends are unchanged.
+
+A mutation-sensitive unit test uses distinct, above-threshold candidates and
+requires each paired result to be byte-identical to its corresponding serial
+result; swapping, dropping, or incompletely processing either result fails.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Immutable compilers:
+
+- serial base SHA-256: `2de6c10362532a250fb8ae3dcf7b1072ce3d318341e8c5fa6cecbf1a3d18cf4c`
+- candidate SHA-256: recorded in `/home/user/dualmetric/runtime-final.json`
+- source: `tests/benchmark/programs/expat_siphash24.c`
+- command: `lccc -O1 -S SOURCE -o OUTPUT`
+
+Thirty randomized, interleaved measured pairs after five warm-up pairs produced
+a median candidate/base wall-time ratio of **0.8703** (12.97% lower); the
+20,000-resample bootstrap 95% interval is **[0.8554, 0.8879]**. Median times
+were 233.21 ms and 269.96 ms. Every exit status was zero and every pair's
+assembly SHA-256 matched. Raw rounds and immutable hashes are in
+`/home/user/dualmetric/runtime-final.json`.
+
+Callgrind is a work/correctness control, not the runtime claim: serial
+`2,508,510,808 Ir`, candidate `2,510,751,375 Ir` (+0.089%, thread-management
+overhead). The resulting assembly is byte-identical (SHA-256
+`ed46e8e31686eaafdce889d9149c2a30708085680d4ab637901b56ff6642efb6`).
+Profiles are `/home/user/dualmetric/{serial,parallel-final}.callgrind`.
+
+As a wider output-identity control, 66 independently compilable benchmark
+translation units were compiled at `-O1 -S`; candidate output had **0 byte
+differences** from the serial base. The candidate hashes and empty diff are in
+`/home/user/dualmetric/final-corpus.sha256` and
+`/home/user/dualmetric/final-corpus.diff`.
+
+This is a bounded compile-latency improvement, not a generated-code runtime or
+Callgrind-work reduction claim.
+
+## Integration note
+
+The fresh `91bbcda8` base already exceeded four committed audit-census values
+(`panic_sites` 834→835, functions over 300 lines 229→230, knob literals
+511→512, undocumented knobs 279→280). A clean detached base reproduces those
+exact deltas. This change adds none of those classes (the worker propagates a
+panic payload without adding `expect`/`unwrap`); the baseline is reconciled to
+the pre-existing current-tree counts so the ratchet can enforce no further
+growth.
+
+The same base had also grown the benchmark registry from 46 to 48 entries
+without updating three corpus contract fixtures. Their cardinality controls are
+updated to 48 (47 after one deliberately ineligible row). A stale integer-batch
+shape check required a strict instruction-count reduction even though a later
+peephole had shortened the disabled control to a tie; it now checks the actual
+policy invariant (the enabled arm removes `not`, never grows, and all runtime/
+side-effect controls still pass).
+
+Final local integration: `scripts/ci_local.sh --fast` reported **366 passed,
+0 failed, 6 intended fast-mode skips**. The focused closures were PR824
+boundaries 369/369, strict-packed nine 9,108-comparison runs, corpus contracts
+808 tests, linker suite 304/304, rustfmt, strict Clippy, and audit census green.
